WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.310
<v Instructor>Okay, so now let's get into S3,</v>

2
00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.620
S3 is gonna be for object storage, it's serverless,

3
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.750
you get unlimited storage, it's pay as you go.

4
00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:09.240
We already know this, it's good for a static content.

5
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.650
You access object using a key

6
00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:12.330
and there is no indexing facility,

7
00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.280
we can use DynamoDB for that.

8
00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.360
It's not a file system,

9
00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.600
it cannot be mounted natively on EC2, so it's good to know.

10
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.040
As an anti-pattern, what is it?

11
00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.140
It's not good to have lots of small files.

12
00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.510
It's not good if you want to have a POSIX file system,

13
00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.030
you need to use EFS instead for POSIX.

14
00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:30.210
It's not good if you want things such as file locking.

15
00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:31.860
It doesn't have a search feature,

16
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.740
queries, and it's not good for rapidly sharing data.

17
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.630
And it's not good if you have a website

18
00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:39.480
that has a lot of dynamic content, okay?

19
00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.760
For the rest, it's a great tool.

20
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.230
So here is a comparison

21
00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:46.110
of all the storage classes on Amazon S3.

22
00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:49.710
So you have Standard, Intelligent-Tiering, Standard-IA,

23
00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.380
One Zone-IA, and then we have three for Glacier,

24
00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.480
we have Glacier Instant Retrieval,

25
00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.410
Glacier Flexible Retrieval, and Glacier Deep Archive.

26
00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:00.720
So you should know all of them by then.

27
00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.450
If you don't, I would suggest you look at the documentation

28
00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:06.330
or a video, for example, in my associate courses

29
00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.280
that will describe all of these.

30
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.720
Now, the one thing you should know though

31
00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.790
around these storage classes that, of course,

32
00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:14.340
you can transition objects between different tiers

33
00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.883
or even delete them using the S3 Lifecycle Policies.

34
00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:20.790
So S3 has a replication feature,

35
00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:23.250
for this you need to enable versioning on your buckets

36
00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:24.690
and you have two kinds of replication.

37
00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:27.570
You have the CRR, Cross Region Replication

38
00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.360
and SRR, Same Region Replication.

39
00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.310
The idea is that an S3 bucket in one region,

40
00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.890
for example, for Cross Region Replication

41
00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:36.480
is going to be replicated

42
00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.090
to another region, that happens very fast.

43
00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.340
You can combine this with Lifecycle Rules, for example,

44
00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.380
so that's in the target bucket,

45
00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:44.400
you have a Lifecycle Rule

46
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.940
to transition data into the S3 Glacier tier.

47
00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:48.960
But as a default,

48
00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.450
the S3 replication does not take into account tiers.

49
00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.360
So you need to manage tiers and Lifecycle Rules

50
00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.520
directly on the target buckets.

51
00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.470
So this helps to reduce latency

52
00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.840
when you want to access your data globally,

53
00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.550
this helps with disaster recovery,

54
00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:04.530
and this helps with security.

55
00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:06.450
There's also another feature you need to know about

56
00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:10.020
called the S3 Replication Time Control, S3 RTC,

57
00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.120
which is a way to guarantee

58
00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.300
that most of the objects that you upload to Amazon S3

59
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:20.300
will be replicated in seconds and 99.99% of those objects

60
00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:22.560
within 15 minutes.

61
00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.520
And in case one object is beyond the 15 minutes threshold,

62
00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:27.870
then you will receive an alarm

63
00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:29.850
thanks to CloudWatch Alarm and a metric.

64
00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:31.380
So it's extremely helpful

65
00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.990
when you have compliance or digital recovery needs

66
00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:36.300
and you want everything to be replicated

67
00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:40.500
at the maximum of 15 minutes between buckets.

68
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.170
Then we have S3 Event Notifications.

69
00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.450
So it is for you to react to events happening

70
00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:46.590
within your S3 buckets

71
00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:50.810
such as S3:ObjectCreated, S3:ObjectRemoved,

72
00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:52.860
S3:ObjectRestore, or S3:Replication.

73
00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.270
You can filter by object name,

74
00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:57.270
for example, you say only the JPEG files I want

75
00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:59.220
and the use cases, for example, would be

76
00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:00.780
to automatically generate thumbnails

77
00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:03.090
of images uploaded to Amazon S3.

78
00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:06.030
You can create as many S3 events as desired

79
00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:06.990
and these notifications

80
00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:09.030
are typically delivered within seconds,

81
00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:11.730
but sometimes can take a minute or longer.

82
00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:13.680
So the events happen to Amazon S3

83
00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:18.333
and then you can trigger SNS, SQS, or a Lambda Function.

84
00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.090
But there's also a second feature

85
00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:22.530
with S3 Event Notifications

86
00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:23.940
that it was going to be leveraging

87
00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:25.260
Amazon EventBridge.

88
00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:28.200
So the idea is that all your events in Amazon S3

89
00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.500
are going to be shared with Amazon EventBridge

90
00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.270
and this is a feature you can enable

91
00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:35.010
and once they are in EventBridge,

92
00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:38.700
then you can set up any kind of very complex rules

93
00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:39.930
within EventBridge

94
00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:44.040
to send to over 18 AWS service destinations.

95
00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.020
So that means that thanks to EventBridge

96
00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.000
we can really increase the power

97
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.460
of the S3 Event Notifications feature.

98
00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:51.780
So thanks to EventBridge,

99
00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:55.320
we can have advanced filtering options with JSON rules,

100
00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:58.770
so before, for just the normal S3 Event Notifications,

101
00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:00.510
you cannot filter by metadata,

102
00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:02.100
object size, object name and so on,

103
00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:05.130
but you can with EventBridge because all that information

104
00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:07.440
is available within EventBridge.

105
00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.920
You can also set up multiple destinations for one rule.

106
00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.470
For example, one rule can send data

107
00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:16.260
to Step Functions and trigger a workflow.

108
00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.420
You can send data directly into Kinesis Streams

109
00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.250
or Firehose and so on.

110
00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.500
And because it is using Amazon EventBridge,

111
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:26.070
then you get more visibility into the archive feature,

112
00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:29.790
you can replay events, and you get more reliable delivery.

113
00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:33.123
So it's definitely an enhancement of S3 notifications.

114
00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.780
Next we get the S3 performance.

115
00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:38.670
So by default,

116
00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:41.790
Amazon S3 will scale automatically to high request rate

117
00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:43.140
and the latency will be between

118
00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:46.080
100 and 200 milliseconds to get the first byte.

119
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.390
Your application can achieve at least

120
00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:51.750
3,500 PUT/COPY/POST/DELETE per second

121
00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:55.110
or 5,500 GET/HEAD requests per second

122
00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:56.940
per prefix in a bucket.

123
00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:57.870
And there's no limit

124
00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:00.090
to how many prefixes you can have in a bucket.

125
00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:02.940
But when you first need to define what is a prefix.

126
00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:04.830
so I'm gonna give you an object path

127
00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:06.990
and I'm gonna give you the prefix out of it.

128
00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:11.990
So if your file is in bucket/folder1/sub1/file,

129
00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:17.360
then your prefix in this example is /folder1/sub1/.

130
00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:21.600
If you have a sub2 file in another directory

131
00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.320
the prefix is going to be /folder1/sub2.

132
00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:28.920
Finally, if your file is into the directory name number one,

133
00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:30.360
then your prefix is one.

134
00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:32.760
And if your file is in prefix number two,

135
00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:34.680
then your prefix is two.

136
00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:36.870
The idea is that for each of these four prefixes

137
00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:39.180
that we have extracted out of the object path,

138
00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.630
we're going to get 3,500 PUT/COPY/POST/DELETE per second

139
00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:45.390
and 5,500 GET/HEAD requests per second.

140
00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:46.650
So that means that

141
00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:49.170
if you want to have a maximum amount of reads,

142
00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:51.210
you can create different prefix for your file

143
00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:53.220
and then you can scale this way.

144
00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:55.740
So if you've spread the reads of that one file

145
00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:59.360
across the four prefixes we have evenly, then we can achieve

146
00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:02.490
22,000 requests per second for GET and HEAD.

147
00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:06.420
And you can do the math for PUT/COPY/POST/DELETE, etc, etc.

148
00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:08.490
So this is for the baseline performance.

149
00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:10.470
Then we have other performance optimizations

150
00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:11.910
we can get on S3.

151
00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.040
The first one is for Multi-Part upload.

152
00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:17.130
This is going to be recommended for files over 100 megabytes

153
00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:20.820
and you must use it for files over five gigabytes.

154
00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:23.130
The idea is that you paralyze the upload

155
00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:24.660
to speed up the transfers

156
00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:27.390
by separating a file into multiple parts,

157
00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:29.310
hence the name Multi-Part.

158
00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:32.490
So our big file has to be sent to Amazon S3.

159
00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:34.260
We're going to divide it into parts,

160
00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:35.490
they're gonna be smaller,

161
00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:38.070
and all these parts are going to be uploaded in parallel

162
00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:39.840
to maximize the bandwidth

163
00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:40.890
and then also make sure

164
00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:44.040
that in case one of these files cannot be uploaded,

165
00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:45.540
we don't have to retry the entire thing,

166
00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:48.300
we can just retry that one specific part.

167
00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:51.150
Now once the entire Multi-Part upload is done,

168
00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:53.970
Amazon S3 will be notified of it

169
00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:57.480
and will reconstruct our bigger file into the bucket

170
00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:00.810
thanks to all the Multi-Part uploads that have been done.

171
00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:03.510
That's number one and that's for upload.

172
00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:05.430
Another performance improvement we can get

173
00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:08.370
is called S3 Transfer Acceleration.

174
00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:10.830
In this case, we increase the transfer speed

175
00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:14.760
by transferring a file first to an AWS edge location,

176
00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:16.200
which will forward the data

177
00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:18.360
to the S3 bucket in the target region,

178
00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:21.210
all using the AWS internal network.

179
00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:24.150
And this is compatible with Multi-Part upload,

180
00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:26.460
so we can combine Multi-Part upload

181
00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:28.980
and S3 Transfer Acceleration.

182
00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:31.350
So the idea is that our file is in the United States

183
00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:34.080
and we are uploading to a bucket in Australia.

184
00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:36.270
Instead of going over the public internet,

185
00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:37.860
we just go over the public internet

186
00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:40.380
to an edge location in the United States

187
00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:42.810
and then Amazon from the edge location

188
00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:45.930
will go over the private AWS network into Australia

189
00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:48.630
and that is supposedly a lot faster

190
00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:50.250
than using the public internet

191
00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:52.530
and possibly a lot cheaper.

192
00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:54.960
So that is performance improvements for uploads.

193
00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:56.730
But what about downloads?

194
00:07:56.730 --> 00:07:59.910
For this, we can use S3 Byte-Range Fetches.

195
00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:02.220
The idea is that we want to receive a big file

196
00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.170
and we have to separate it into parts

197
00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:06.270
and so it's ranges of bytes.

198
00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:08.010
And so we're going to paralyze the GETs

199
00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:11.790
by requesting specific byte ranges in parallel.

200
00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:14.280
As well in case one of these GETs fails,

201
00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:16.350
we get better resilience in case of failure

202
00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:19.650
because we only need to request the one part, the one range

203
00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:20.550
that has failed.

204
00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:23.130
So they can be used to speed up downloads.

205
00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:25.380
So second example, our file is in S3

206
00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:27.450
and we're going to have the first part, the second part,

207
00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:30.360
and the Nth part all requested in parallel.

208
00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:31.470
And in case one of these fail,

209
00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:34.170
we can just re-request that one part as we need.

210
00:08:34.170 --> 00:08:36.390
So this is going to speed up definitely

211
00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:37.980
your GET performance because

212
00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:41.250
we are gonna have a much higher bandwidth utilization.

213
00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:42.420
But this can also be used

214
00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:44.730
if you want to retrieve only partial data.

215
00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:47.160
For example, if you want to retrieve the head of a file

216
00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:49.560
and by head of a file, I mean the first few bytes.

217
00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:51.300
So in case you have a file in S3

218
00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:52.830
and you want to retrieve the header,

219
00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:54.720
you're going to do a Byte-range request for the header

220
00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:56.850
for the whatever bytes you need

221
00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:58.890
and this will give you the part of the file you need.

222
00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:01.860
So this saves you a lot of cost, bandwidth, and so on,

223
00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:03.570
and you can just look at what you need

224
00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:06.420
to retrieve that one specific header.

225
00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:08.400
Next we learn about a feature to

226
00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:11.100
remove incomplete parts for Multi-Part Upload.

227
00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:12.570
So when we have a big file,

228
00:09:12.570 --> 00:09:15.420
we know we can use the Multi-Part Upload feature

229
00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:18.840
to upload them part by part into an S3 buckets.

230
00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:21.360
So what will happen is that there will be parallel uploads

231
00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.010
into the S3 buckets,

232
00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:24.660
but it is possible for you to

233
00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:26.880
stop the parallel uploads halfway

234
00:09:26.880 --> 00:09:28.890
and therefore you will have on your S3 buckets

235
00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:31.020
some parts that are going to be missing

236
00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:33.660
and some parts that will just sit there and do nothing.

237
00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:36.870
So it's possible for you to set up an S3 Lifecycle Policy

238
00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:41.610
to say that if there is an abortion of the

239
00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:44.130
parallel upload and the parts

240
00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:46.560
haven't been touched for say seven days,

241
00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:48.900
then abort the Multi-Part Upload

242
00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:51.720
and clean, delete the all files.

243
00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:53.250
So then it will be deleted.

244
00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:56.595
And for this you can also initiate it with, for example,

245
00:09:56.595 --> 00:10:01.530
an CLI API call to list and complete Multi-Part Uploads.

246
00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:02.610
So that's it for this lecture,

247
00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:05.560
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

